WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
"Best Temperature, Timing, and Duration
for Cold Plunges for Athletic Performance Recovery"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.480
In the last decade,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.640
cold-water immersion
has emerged

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.840
as one of the most popular

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.560
post-exercise
recovery strategies

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.240
utilized among athletes
during training and competition.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.520
This primarily stems
from the belief

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.960
that cold-water immersion

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.720
facilitates aspects
of recovery and regeneration,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.480
thereby conferring
a potential training

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.320
and performance advantage.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.680
But is it a friend, foe,
or just futile?

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.720
The cold truth...
is that local cryotherapy,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.000
meaning like an ice pack,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.000
does not appear
to diminish soreness

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.160
or accelerate
the recovery strength.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.960
Local cryotherapy
does not seem to contribute

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.000
to the improvement
of delayed onset muscle soreness

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.280
or muscle weakness

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.320
associated with
exercise-induced muscle damage,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.760
but what about cold plunges?

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.920
If you look at
the largest systematic review

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.160
and meta-analysis
of the effects of cold plunges

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.640
on the recovery
of physical performance,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.760
compiling the results
of more than five dozen

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.320
randomized, controlled,
or crossover studies,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.400
after cold-water immersion,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.120
people felt less sore
after a workout,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.680
at one hour later,
six hours later,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.640
one day, two days,
and three days later,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.720
but not after four days.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.200
They also felt less fatigued,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.880
particularly in the hour
or hours immediately afterwards.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.480
And, after three days,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.520
they reported feeling
more recovered

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.800
than those who hadn’t done
the cold plunges.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.200
But were
they actually more recovered

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.360
using objective measures?

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.800
Endurance was significantly
improved an hour later,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.320
but not after that.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.160
Jump performance was impaired
six hours after the cold plunge

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.040
but was significantly improved
24 hours later.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.200
Allow me a quick tangent
on heterogeneity.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.000
See this i squared column?

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.920
This is something
I don’t talk about enough.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.280
i squared is a number
between 0 and 100

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.760
and represents
the amount of variation

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.320
between studies that’s due
to true differences in effect

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.960
rather than just due to chance.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.600
So, it’s a measure
of inconsistency.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.440
The 0 percent here means that
there was perfect consistency;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.960
so, any difference
in the results

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.280
was due just to random chance.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.680
A low value like that
gives us more confidence

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.080
that we’re dealing
with a real effect.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.000
But the 44 percent here
suggests more variability.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.240
Check out
the individual studies.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.560
Here are the results
of 29 studies

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.120
on the recovery
of muscular power performance

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.360
after high-intensity exercise.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.600
If we put
all the studies together,

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.760
there may be a small benefit
favoring cold plunges,

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.280
but only about one in five

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.520
found statistically
significant benefit,

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.640
one found harm,

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.840
and the vast majority
found no significant effect.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.440
Still, enough of the studies

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.880
leaned in the direction
of benefit to tip the scale.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.960
In terms of sprint performance,

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.840
there was an initial
deterioration in performance,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.200
but then none after that,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.760
and a significant increase
in strength recovery

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.280
the next day.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.800
This coincides with a lessening
of muscular damage indicators

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.880
the next day, lasting through
days two and three.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.440
What are the best parameters
of cold plunges

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.400
in terms of timing,
temperature, and duration?

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.800
There really isn’t
a "gold standard,"

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.560
but the first systematic review

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.720
concluded that
cold-water immersion

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.440
at a temperature
between 11 and 15°C

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.960
for 11 15 minutes was best.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.360
That’s like
in the 50s Fahrenheit.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.200
However,
since that first review,

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.560
there’s been
a bunch more studies,

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.320
and the updated review

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.680
found that the water temperature
didn’t seem to matter,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.200
whether severely cold at

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.680
5 to 9 degrees Celsius
(in the Fahrenheit 40s)

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.040
or that moderately cold
at 10 to 15°C,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.360
but the immersion duration
did seem to matter.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.840
Immersions lasting
less than ten minutes

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.440
presented better results

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.760
and plunging
for more than 16 minutes

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.440
didn’t seem to help at all.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.320
And it’s better to immerse
immediately following exercise

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.320
rather than waiting.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.080
Now, in most of these studies,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.560
researchers used
passive recovery

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:25.520
in the control groups,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.680
meaning people were randomized
to cold plunges

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.520
or to just, like, sit
in a chair for 20 minutes.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.520
But who exercises intensely
and then just sits in a chair?

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.840
Many athletes use
active recovery

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.120
in the form
of a low-intensity "warm down"

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.960
to recover after exercise,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.920
like walking around a bit
to keep the blood flowing,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.640
getting rid of some
of that lactic acid buildup.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:49.600
So, instead,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.320
what about randomizing people
to cold-water immersion

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.280
versus normal active recovery?

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.760
That might be
a fairer comparison.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.240
And in that case,
the cold plunge didn’t work.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.800
It was no more effective
than active recovery.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.320
Okay, but that’s just one study.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.280
Let’s look at a meta-analysis
of all such studies

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.200
comparing the effects
of cold-water immersion

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.840
with other recovery modalities.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.520
In terms of muscle soreness,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.720
cold-water immersion
didn’t seem to work any better

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.800
than active recovery or any
of the other recovery methods,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.960
including warm-water immersion.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.600
Same for the recovery
of muscular power.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.200
No significant difference

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.600
between cold plunging
and active recovery,

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.960
contrast water therapy,

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.280
which is like alternating
hot and cold,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.560
warm-water immersion,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.360
air cryotherapy,
like in a cryochamber,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.960
massage,
or electrical stimulation.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.800
If it’s all the same,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.400
I think I might prefer
the massage to the plunge.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.280
Same for the recovery of muscle
strength, no difference.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.120
Immersing in cold water
did seem to beat out warm water

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.200
on perceived recovery, though,
but nothing else,

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.840
and it was the same with
flexibility, no difference,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.840
and the same with measures
of muscle damage,

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.440
though electrifying your legs

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.480
did seem to work better
than the cold water.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.280
The bottom line
is that all the recovery methods

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.680
seem to achieve about the same.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.600
So, a cold plunge
may be better

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.400
than just sitting down
after intense exercise,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.480
but there’s a wide range
of other practices

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.200
that appear to be
just as effective.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.080
But any time doing something
beats out doing nothing,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.040
you always have to ask if it
might just be a placebo effect.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.760
You don’t know
if the perceived benefits

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.000
of cold plunges
are all in your head

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.040
until you put it to the test

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.240
with a placebo-controlled trial,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.720
but how do you even do that?

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.660
We’ll find out, next.

